<!-- ===================== SECTION 15: ADVENT OF EUROPEANS ===================== -->
<h2 id="advent-europeans">15. Advent of Europeans (1498-1765)</h2>

<p>The <span class="highlight">Advent of Europeans</span> in India began with the arrival of the Portuguese navigator <strong>Vasco da Gama</strong> off the coast of Calicut (Kerala) on 20 May 1498. The Portuguese were followed by the Dutch (1602), the British East India Company (1600 — but they entered India in 1608), the Danish (1616), and the French (1664). The competition between these European trading companies over the lucrative spice and textile trade of India eventually led to the <strong>British conquest of India</strong> after the <strong>Battle of Plassey</strong> (1757) and the <strong>Battle of Buxar</strong> (1764), which gave the British East India Company effective control over Bengal, Bihar, and Odisha — the richest provinces of the Mughal Empire.</p>

<h3>Portuguese (1498-1961)</h3>

<p>The Portuguese were the first Europeans to establish a colonial presence in India. Vasco da Gama's voyage around the Cape of Good Hope broke the Venetian-Arab monopoly on the spice trade. The Portuguese established trading stations/factories at <strong>Calicut</strong> (1498), <strong>Cochin</strong> (1500), <strong>Goa</strong> (1510 — captured by Afonso de Albuquerque who established it as the capital of Portuguese India), <strong>Diu</strong> (1535 — acquired through the Treaty of Bassein with Bahadur Shah of Gujarat), <strong>Daman</strong> (1559), <strong>Mumbai</strong> (1534 — Bassein region, but they traded a port to the English later as dowry: Bombay was given to the British as part of the dowry of Catherine of Braganza in 1661), <strong>Hugli</strong> (Bengal — 1579), and <strong>Nagapatam</strong> (Coromandel Coast). The Portuguese viceroy was based at Goa — the <strong>Old Goa</strong> became a magnificent city (Churches of Bom Jesus, St. Francis of Assisi, etc.). The Portuguese enforced their monopoly of the spice trade by requiring all Indian ships to carry cartazes (licenses) and attacking any ship without one. They also introduced the <strong>Inquisition</strong> in Goa (1560 — to persecute converted Christians suspected of backsliding to Hinduism). The Portuguese declined as a naval power in India due to their inability to compete with the English and Dutch (more capital, better ships, better organisation) and the Spanish annexation of Portugal (1580-1640). Their remaining Indian possessions (Goa, Daman, Diu) were invaded by India in 1961 (Operation Vijay).</p>

<h3>Dutch (1602-1825)</h3>

<p>The <strong>Dutch East India Company (VOC)</strong> was established in 1602 — the first multinational corporation in history, with a charter to wage war, negotiate treaties, and govern territories. The Dutch captured the Portuguese forts in Ceylon (Sri Lanka) and established their Indian factories at <strong>Pulicat</strong> (1610 — north of Madras, their main centre until 1690), <strong>Surat</strong> (1616), <strong>Brimore</strong> (Travancore/Cochin region — 1663; captured from the Portuguese), <strong>Nagapatam</strong> (1658, captured from the Portuguese), <strong>Sadras</strong>, <strong>Tranquebar</strong> (temporarily), <strong>Chinsura</strong> (near Hugli, Bengal — 1653), <strong>Baleshwar</strong> (Odisha), and <strong>Patna</strong> (Bihar). Their main exports were spices (pepper, cinnamon, cloves, nutmeg), silk, cotton (Coromandel coast), indigo, saltpetre (for gunpowder), and opium. The Dutch were the most powerful European trading company in the 17th century, but they were more interested in the Indonesian (Spice Islands/Archipelago) trade than India. They were defeated by the British in the <strong>Anglo-Dutch Wars</strong> (1780-84 — the British captured Dutch settlements in India, and the Dutch were finally expelled from India by 1825).</p>

<h3>British East India Company (1600-1858)</h3>

<p>The <strong>English East India Company (EIC)</strong> was founded on 31 December 1600 by Queen Elizabeth I's royal charter. It entered India in 1608 (Captain William Hawkins visited Jahangir's court to seek permission for trade at Surat). Permission was granted in 1613 after the embassy of Sir Thomas Roe (1615-1619). The EIC established factories at <strong>Surat</strong> (1613 — the first factory), <strong>Masulipatnam</strong> (1611 — the first on the Coromandel Coast), <strong>Fort St. George</strong> (Madras/Chennai — 1644, became the EIC's first major fortified settlement, founded by Francis Day), <strong>Bombay</strong> (1661 — as dowry of Catherine of Braganza to Charles II; the EIC leased it for £10/year), <strong>Fort William</strong> (Calcutta/Kolkata — 1690, founded by Job Charnock; the EIC built the Maratha Ditch and Fort William after the Siraj-ud-Daula attack in 1756). The EIC's rise to political power in India began with the <strong>Battle of Plassey</strong> (23 June 1757) — Robert Clive, with 3,000 troops (including Indian sepoys), defeated Siraj-ud-Daula, the Nawab of Bengal (whose troops numbered 50,000 but were commanded by traitor Mir Jafar, who had secretly allied with the British). This was not a battle but a coup — most of Siraj's army never fought. After this victory, the British installed Mir Jafar as Nawab of Bengal (who gave them the zamindari of 24 Parganas) and effectively controlled the richest province of India. The <strong>Battle of Buxar</strong> (22 October 1764) — the EIC under Hector Munro defeated the combined army of Mir Qasim (Nawab of Bengal), Shuja-ud-Daulah (Nawab of Awadh), and Shah Alam II (the Mughal Emperor). As a result, the EIC obtained the <strong>Diwani</strong> (revenue rights) of Bengal, Bihar, and Odisha (Treaty of Allahabad, 1765). This gave the Company the legal right to collect taxes, transforming it from a trading company into a sovereign power.</p>

<h3>French (1664-1793)</h3>

<p>The <strong>French East India Company</strong> (Compagnie Francaise des Indes Orientales) was founded by Jean-Baptiste Colbert (Louis XIV's finance minister) in 1664. The French established factories at <strong>Surat</strong> (1668), <strong>Masulipatnam</strong> (1669), <strong>Pondicherry</strong> (1673 — the capital of French India; the Governor General resided here), <strong>Chandernagore</strong> (Bengal, 1690), <strong>Mahe</strong> (Malabar Coast, 1721), <strong>Yanam</strong> (Andhra, 1723), <strong>Karaikal</strong> (Tamil Nadu, 1739). The French were the main rivals of the British in the <strong>Carnatic Wars</strong> (1746-1763). The French governor <strong>Dupleix</strong> (1742-1754) allied with Indian princes (the Nizam of Hyderabad, Chanda Sahib of Carnatic) and used Indian troops trained in European methods (the first to do so) — the institution of the 'sepoy' army. The British under Robert Clive defeated the French at the <strong>Battle of Arcot</strong> (1751) and the <strong>Battle of Wandiwash</strong> (1760 — decisive victory by Sir Eyre Coote over the French comte de Lally). By the Treaty of Paris (1763), the French were restricted to their five Indian settlements (Pondicherry, Chandernagore, Mahe, Yanam, Karaikal) without any military presence. The French never recovered their political influence in India.</p>

<h3>The Carnatic Wars (1746-1763)</h3>

<p>The <strong>Carnatic Wars</strong> established British supremacy over the French in India. <strong>First Carnatic War</strong> (1746-1748) — part of the Austrian War of Succession in Europe. The French captured Madras (1746), but the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle (1748) returned it to the British. <strong>Second Carnatic War</strong> (1749-1754) — a proxy war: Dupleix supported Chanda Sahib (claimant to Carnatic) and Muzaffar Jang (claimant to Hyderabad), while the British supported Anwar-ud-Din (the existing Nawab of Carnatic) and Nasir Jang (the existing Nizam). The British under Clive captured Arcot (1751) and defeated the French, leading to Dupleix's recall. <strong>Third Carnatic War</strong> (1758-1763) — part of the Seven Years' War in Europe. The French captured Fort St. David but were defeated at the decisive <strong>Battle of Wandiwash</strong> (1760). The Treaty of Paris (1763) confined the French to their five settlements, ending their imperial ambitions in India.</p>

<div class="diagram-box"><pre>
  EUROPEAN PRESENCE IN INDIA (1650-1750)
  
  PORTUGUESE: Goa (1510), Daman, Diu, Bassein
  DUTCH: Pulicat (1610), Chinsura, Nagapatam
  BRITISH: Surat (1613), Madras (1644), Bombay (1661), Calcutta (1690)
  FRENCH: Pondicherry (1673), Chandernagore (1690), Mahe
  DANISH: Tranquebar (1620), Serampore (1755)
  
  → By 1765, British control Bengal, Bihar, Odisha
  → By 1818, British control all of India (except Kashmir, Hyderabad)
</pre></div>

